    {
      "name": "resolution_time_hours",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.98,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "This is the target variable for the query -- it directly quantifies how long incidents take, enabling all grouping/modelling. Derivable from opened_at and closed_at, both present. The -1 sentinel (128 rows) is a reasonable 'unresolved/unknown' marker. Values appear consistent with the timestamps, so groundedness is high."
    },
    {
      "name": "priority_level",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.15,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "An original 'priority' column already exists; this appears to be a trivial numeric restatement, adding little new analytical leverage. Values are grounded (a direct read of priority)."
    },
    {
      "name": "category_criticality",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Mapping category to a criticality tier gives a genuine new axis to compare resolution time against. However the criticality assignment is an inferred judgement not explicitly present; the evidence samples do not even show the source category, so the specific 1/2/3 assignment is only weakly warranted from the text and risks imposed rankings."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_weekend_opened",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.8,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Weekend-vs-weekday opening is a plausible driver of longer resolution, offering a clean new grouping axis. Derivable from opened_at. One sample flagged 2023-01-02 (a Monday) as weekend=1, which is questionable, suggesting some derivation inaccuracy, but the concept is well grounded."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.72,
  "source_groundedness": 0.79,
  "rationale": "The set provides the core target metric (resolution_time_hours) plus two genuinely useful explanatory axes (weekend timing, category criticality) that support the 'why do some take longer' analysis. priority_level is largely redundant with the existing priority column. Most values are soundly derivable from present timestamp/category fields, though category_criticality involves an unshown inferred ranking and at least one weekend flag looks mis-derived, mildly lowering groundedness."
}

